Can the owner run at 100 MHz?  The command can: DM_CMD_SET_FREQUENCY -f 100,400 selects mode 62 (1100 MHz / 11) of the step clock; the handler is identical in 0.18.0, 0.20.0 and 0.21.0 (the public source nearest card 0's 0.21.2); it changes no voltage [source].  No lab card has run below 300 MHz (beyond the microseconds each PLL4 change spends bypassed to the 100 MHz reference: inference from io_pll.c), so whether the minions, the shire DLL and the master minion's runtime (its handshake timeouts, 5-10 timer ticks, are 52-105 ms of wall time if a tick is the 10.5 ms that the measured ~1.05 s heartbeat implies, review-governor.md section 1.4) work there is not established [inference].  aifoundry3 is the natural card (its governor is latched); on aifoundry2 the governor restores 600 MHz at its next idle or thermal-loop exit unless the owner changes it; card 1 must not have its voltages set; card 0 is off-limits [source].
Thermally, 100 MHz alone does not make a bare card safe: see the lines above.  With the heatsink on, every settable clock settles at idle, and a sustained 16-shire pattern settles below 85 C in >= 90 % of draws at: a2 no settable clock with the voltages left, 300 MHz at the floor; a3 300 MHz with the voltages left, 400 MHz at the floor; c1 200 MHz with the voltages left, 400 MHz at the floor [model]
10 MHz: no PLL mode exists; it needs a new BL2 (signed and flashed; the cards have never run one) or a debugger [source].  At the floor voltages the minion and SRAM clock tree left at 100 MHz is only 0.118 / 0.201 / 0.266 W on aifoundry2's die; 10 MHz would remove nine tenths of it, 0.123 / 0.209 / 0.276 W at the board with its regulators' loss (NoC lowered too): everything left is leakage and power no clock touches [model].
What ten times slower buys: the clock can be set to 100 MHz (6x slower; 60 MHz, 10x, is not in the table).  At idle it removes the clock tree, 1.06 / 1.87 / 2.45 W of aifoundry2's idle at 60 C (5th/50th/95th), and nothing of the leakage, which the voltage and the die temperature set.  Under a 16-shire pattern the switching falls from 11.3 / 12.8 / 14.2 W at the board at 600 MHz to 1.89 / 2.13 / 2.36 W with the voltages left, 1.12 / 1.26 / 1.4 W at the floor: the load stops mattering, the idle floor does not.  The camera's signal falls by the same factor, and the lock-in time a pattern needs grows as 1/signal^2: 36 times at 100 MHz with the voltages left, 103 times at the floor; on a taped lid most patterns still show within 10-40 s at 100 MHz (section H) [model].
A short look on a bare, taped lid (aifoundry2): lock-in seconds for SNR 3 against the look a bare card leaves, from the low point being set (20-60 s after the boot) to the plan's 75 C stop with the 16-shire pattern running (median, and the share with none left), and, for comparison, the seconds to 85 C had the point been set at the instant of boot (median) [model]:
   600 MHz boot      : lock-in checkerboard <=10 s, one shire <=10 s, 2x2 block moving <=10 s, half die <=10 s; look to 75 C still 12 s (18 % none), fan 18 s (9 % none); set at boot, to 85 C still 20 s, fan 28 s
   300 MHz floor     : lock-in checkerboard <=10 s, one shire <=10 s, 2x2 block moving <=10 s, half die <=10 s; look to 75 C still 0 s (63 % none), fan 21 s (39 % none); set at boot, to 85 C still 53 s, fan 102 s
   100 MHz floor     : lock-in checkerboard 29 s, one shire <=10 s, 2x2 block moving <=10 s, half die <=10 s; look to 75 C still 0 s (63 % none), fan 42 s (39 % none); set at boot, to 85 C still 70 s, fan 164 s
